$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(\frac{5\pi}{12}\right) &= \tan\left(\frac{2\pi}{3} - \frac{\pi}{4}\right) \\
&= \frac{\tan\left(\frac{2\pi}{3}\right) - \tan\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)}{1 + \tan\left(\frac{2\pi}{3}\right)\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)} \\
&= \frac{\left(-\sqrt{3}\right) - \left(1\right)}{1 + \left(-\sqrt{3}\right)\left(1\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Combining the terms gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(\frac{5\pi}{12}\right) &= \frac{-1 - \sqrt{3}}{1 - \sqrt{3}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiply by the conjugate of the denominator, expand, and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tan\left(\frac{5\pi}{12}\right) &= \frac{-1 - \sqrt{3}}{1 - \sqrt{3}}\cdot \frac{1 + \sqrt{3}}{1 + \sqrt{3}} \\
&= \frac{\left(-1 - \sqrt{3}\right)\left(1 + \sqrt{3}\right)}{\left(1 - \sqrt{3}\right)\left(1 + \sqrt{3}\right)} \\
&= \frac{-4 - 2\sqrt{3}}{-2} \\
&= 2 + \sqrt{3}
\end{aligned}
